# Edit and board status isolation

Private engine issue 90 reports a deployed TypeScript copy answering 200 for
signed-out edit/board requests where retained live responses answer 403.
The reported deployed identity is not the engine integration checkpoint.
No live requests or deployed configuration changes were made here.

## Current-source observations

The actual SDK-free `handleEngineRequest` was exercised in process at
`7c24b1dfa4f5b5c17b4e942b8dfbddd96a06872c`, using the existing synthetic
page-denial seed and owned RAM SQLite. The six first observations were:

| Synthetic card | Request | Status | Observation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Readable Thing | ordinary page | 200 | Readable body; no denial |
| Readable Thing | edit | 403 | Denial; no form |
| Readable Thing | board | 403 | Denial; no form |
| Secret Thing | ordinary page | 403 | No secret body |
| Secret Thing | edit | 403 | No secret body or form |
| Secret Thing | board | 403 | No secret body or form |

A separately seeded policy control added an explicit `*all+*update` rule
granting `Anyone`, including its real pointer reference, before environment
and codename boot. Readable Thing's edit and board routes then both answered
200 and contained forms. Without that grant both answered 403 with denial
and no forms. This proves that this fixture's request path distinguishes the
two policies; it does not identify the deployed copy's effective policy.

## Retained regression and limits

The existing page-status suite now retains eight additional cases covering
edit/board, granted/ungranted update and normal/request-local anonymous render
paths. Together with its five existing page/read-denial cases, the focused
invocation ran 13 tests, passed 13 and failed/skipped/todo 0. This is local
synthetic source verification, not a site score or a Ruby differential grade.
No product source or permission policy changed.

Exact source hashes, original and policy-control observations, complete native
Vitest JSON reports, environment and cache/retry/model limits are retained in
[the evidence file](crud-view-status-isolation-2026-10-30.json). Each owned
database was closed in `finally`; no server, browser or external effect ran.
Registered ignored `.runtime/issue90-route-probe/` and
`.runtime/issue90-policy-probe/` retain the initial generated probes and logs.

The issue remains open. The next causal check is to pin the installed engine
bundle, actual request/viewer path and scrubbed rule data, then inspect the
affected cards' effective read/update rules. A raw 200/403 difference alone
does not establish that the current generic engine ignores task permissions.
Do not change update rules or weaken a gate to improve a comparison score.
Keep live-site 0.19.1 evidence separate from the existing pinned
Decko 0.20.0/card 1.110.0 reference.
